<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dawachi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1759">1759</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dawachi was the last khan of the Dzungar Khanate, a grandson of the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsering-dondub">Tsering Dondub</a>. In 1753, with the help of the Khoid prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amursana">Amursana</a>, he killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lama-dorji">Lama Dorji</a> and seized power, but the two soon fell out and Amursana went over to the Qing. When a Qing army entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungaria">Dzungaria</a> in 1755 Dawachi fled south, was captured and was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, where he lived under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qianlong-emperor">Qianlong Emperor</a>'s protection until his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dawachi.jpg" alt="Dawachi" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean Denis Attiret · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dawachi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
